# PAN-OS Tunnel Builder

PAN-OS Tunnel Builder creates and migrates route-based IPsec VPNs on Palo Alto
Networks firewalls managed by Panorama. The operator selects a spoke firewall
and a hub firewall by hostname or serial number. The tool discovers their
assigned template stacks, shows which templates contain network configuration,
recommends an eligible template for each endpoint, and confirms the selections
before building configuration.

## LSVPN replacement workflow

When the spoke has GlobalProtect Large Scale VPN (LSVPN) satellite
configuration, the tool discovers the effective hub and spoke configuration and
builds an equivalent standard IPsec replacement plan. Satellite enrollment may
be authorized by username or group, so a spoke serial number is not required in
the hub portal configuration. Hub gateways are matched by configured name or by
endpoint identity, including IPv4, IPv6, and publicly resolvable FQDN values.

Each replacement consists of paired hub and spoke objects for:

- IKEv2 and IPsec crypto profiles with moderate interoperability-oriented
  cryptography.
- IKE gateways and route-based auto-key IPsec tunnels.
- IKE local endpoints validated against effective-running interface addresses.
  The generated gateway uses the raw configured template reference—including a
  Panorama variable such as `$eth2-IP`—and the unique interface that owns its
  resolved effective address.
- A cryptographically random pre-shared key generated independently for each
  replacement tunnel.
- Unique numbered `tunnel.N` interfaces on both endpoints.
- Unique IPv4 `/30` or IPv6 `/127` interface addresses allocated from an
  operator-supplied pool without overlapping existing interface networks. The
  build command prompts for this pool when `--tunnel-address-pool` is omitted.
- Matching routing-instance and zone membership, static routes, and tunnel
  monitoring behavior.

## Shared spoke templates

The template that actually owns the LSVPN satellite is treated as the shared
configuration boundary, independently of the interface-bearing template the
operator initially selects. The tool discovers every template stack that
contains the owner and every managed firewall assigned through those stacks.
All inheriting spokes are migrated as one batch; deleting the shared satellite
for only one firewall is not permitted. Satellite objects owned by multiple
templates must be migrated as separate groups.

The batch must be structurally compatible: satellite names, old and replacement
tunnel-interface layout, vsys, zone, virtual/logical router and VRF, and the
BGP/OSPF/static-routing topology must match. Value differences are represented
with variables. Structural differences stop the migration and identify the
firewall that needs a separate template or migration group.

One reusable IKE gateway and IPsec tunnel are created in the shared LSVPN
template. Tunnel-interface, routing, zone, and import changes are written once
per distinct source-owning network-template placement, so stacks that share the
LSVPN template but use different network templates are handled independently.
Generated device-specific IP fields,
IKE identities, route destinations, and PSKs use deterministic Panorama
variables with per-device template-stack overrides. The hub retains a unique
endpoint and unique variables for every spoke. Hub endpoint names contain the
sanitized hub and spoke hostnames followed by a deterministic three-digit
collision suffix, for example `labPAN3-to-labPAN1-638`; the matching IKE gateway
adds `-ike`. A shared spoke template cannot vary an entry name per device, so
its common object identifies the hub and `shared-spokes` instead of falsely
naming one member. Variable names spell out their role, such as
`$ptb-spoke-tunnel-interface-address-<id>` and
`$ptb-hub-ike-pre-shared-key-<id>`. One random PSK is generated per spoke and
assigned to both its spoke override and matching hub variable. Non-secret
SHA-256 pairing fingerprints are stored as companion
variables for auditability, and deterministic ownership-marker variables
distinguish tool-managed variable sets from unrelated variables with colliding
names. Because Panorama does not expose PSK plaintext for comparison, every
rerun rotates each PSK and writes its spoke and hub values together in the same
candidate transaction.

Candidate loading is all-or-nothing. Variables and their overrides are written
inside the same candidate transaction as the replacement objects. A write or
post-write verification failure issues Panorama's candidate rollback and local
pre-route artifacts from that failed attempt are removed. A successful rerun
rotates paired PSK variables; a failed run leaves no variable or PSK state for
the next attempt. If no LSVPN satellite configuration remains, a load request
is treated as a successful no-op.

The migration preserves dynamic routing when it is detected on the LSVPN path.
This includes legacy virtual routers and advanced-routing logical-router VRFs,
BGP and OSPFv2, BGP peer-group policy and filtering references, and OSPF
interface attributes. Supported topologies include:

- BGP bound directly to the LSVPN tunnel interface.
- OSPF on the tunnel providing reachability between loopback addresses while a
  multihop BGP session carries production routes. This behavior is used only
  when reciprocal loopback peers are detected; direct tunnel-bound BGP takes
  precedence.
- BGP routing policy that advertises loopback prefixes, including link-local
  addresses such as `169.254.65.x`, over a tunnel-bound BGP session.

If reciprocal loopback BGP peers are present without OSPF on the LSVPN tunnel,
the tool preserves the existing loopback-sourced BGP sessions and creates
reciprocal `/32` or `/128` static host routes to the peer addresses through the
new tunnel interfaces. This replaces loopback reachability that LSVPN had been
installing implicitly without rebinding BGP and changing its source address.
Existing per-spoke static routes with the same destination and the old LSVPN
interface are removed on both endpoints; shared hub LSVPN tunnel and gateway
configuration remains intact. Unrelated routes through the old interface still
block candidate loading.
Candidate loading is blocked only when a peer address cannot be resolved to an
IP host route.

## Route validation

The route table and effective routing configuration must be captured before and
after a migration on every affected hub and spoke firewall. Snapshots include
all discovered legacy virtual routers
and advanced-routing logical-router VRFs, IPv4 and IPv6 routes, protocol,
administrative distance, metric, selected and installed state, and ECMP
next-hop/interface paths, plus the virtual-router and logical-router
configuration sections. Volatile values such as route uptime are omitted.

Post-change validation reports missing, added, and changed routes and fails when
the normalized tables do not match. Explicit prefix, tunnel-interface, and
next-hop mappings account for the expected replacement link changes without
hiding unrelated routing drift. Post-change validation should occur only after
BGP and OSPF have converged. A candidate load writes a non-secret migration
validation manifest beside the baseline snapshots. In a later tool run,
`panos-tunnel-builder routes verify-migration MANIFEST` captures both endpoints
again and applies only the tunnel-specific mappings recorded in that manifest.
New connected/local routes for the replacement link and temporary additional
equal-prefix candidates are tolerated, while unrelated route additions or loss
still fail validation. The verifier also removes only the exact planned old/new
interface references and replaced static-route names from its configuration
comparison; any other routing-configuration change fails validation.

## Greenfield workflow

When no existing VPN is present, the intended workflow is to locate interfaces
in external, untrust, or internet zones and create tunnels between those
interfaces and the selected hub. If multiple eligible external interfaces are
present, the tool creates a corresponding redundant tunnel for each one.

## Panorama placement and safety

Generated network objects belong in the Panorama templates that own each
firewall's network configuration. The tool preserves the order of templates in
the assigned stack, identifies templates containing eligible interface
configuration, and allows the operator to override the recommended selection.

The default workflow performs Panorama discovery and generates a local preview.
With the explicit `--load-candidate` option, the tool acquires a temporary
Panorama configuration lock and first verifies that candidate configuration
matches running configuration. A dirty candidate is rejected by default because
the failure rollback is global. The explicit `--allow-dirty-candidate` option
bypasses that protection with a warning. A clean-candidate run saves a
timestamped `LSVPN_PRE_<id>_<timestamp>.xml` Panorama snapshot; an explicitly
allowed dirty-candidate run labels it `LSVPN_CHK_<id>_<timestamp>.xml` to make
clear that it is a checkpoint containing pre-existing pending changes (both
names fit PAN-OS's 32-character saved-config limit). The tool saves hub and
spoke route snapshots locally, and loads the replacement into candidate
configuration. It creates the new hub and spoke objects in the templates that
own the corresponding source configuration, removes the spoke satellite
tunnel and its obsolete interface/routing bindings, and leaves all hub LSVPN
configuration intact. Before removing the old spoke interface, it audits every
template in the spoke stack and blocks on any interface dependency the migration
does not explicitly understand. After writing, it rereads candidate
configuration to verify the replacement object trees, confirm every intended
spoke cleanup, and prove that the hub LSVPN configuration fingerprint is
unchanged. Unrelated name and variable-type collisions are rejected. Reruns
update objects and variables carrying the tool's ownership markers and rotate
both ends of every PSK pair.

PAN-OS interface imports under `vsys/import/network/interface` are migrated as
part of that workflow. Each replacement tunnel interface is imported into the
same endpoint vsys in the source-owning template, and the obsolete spoke tunnel
import is removed with the old satellite interface.

The tool does not commit Panorama, push template stacks, or commit managed
firewalls. The operator performs those steps separately, captures post-change
routes after convergence, and runs the manifest-based verifier. The named
Panorama snapshot can be loaded back into candidate configuration with the
separate restore command; committing and pushing that rollback remains an
operator-controlled action.

Candidate loading is transactional after the rollback snapshot is saved. Any
subsequent object write, spoke cleanup, or post-write verification error causes
the tool to issue Panorama's candidate rollback command before releasing its
configuration lock. This restores candidate configuration to running config
without a commit; it also discards other pending candidate changes. The named
snapshot remains available for manual recovery but is not loaded automatically.
If Panorama rejects the rollback, the command reports both failures and warns
that partial candidate changes may remain.
